ent combinations.\n4. Linearity: The relationship between the factors and the response should be linear.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe 2K factorial design is applicable when:\n- The researcher aims to study multiple factors simultaneously.\n- The factors can be controlled and set at two distinct levels.\n- The interactions between factors are of interest.\nIt is particularly useful in preliminary studies to identify significant factors and interactions before conducting more detailed experiments.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of a 2K factorial design typically involves:\n1. Conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of main effects and interactions.\n2. Estimating the effects using the method of least squares.\n3. Performing post-hoc tests if necessary to explore differences between specific treatment combinations.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Efficiently evaluates multiple factors and their interactions in a single experiment.\n- Provides a comprehensive understanding of the system under study.\n- Facilitates the identification of significant factors and interactions, guiding further experimentation.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The design can become complex and unwieldy as the number of factors increases, leading to a large number of experimental runs.\n- Assumes linearity and may not perform well if the true relationship is nonlinear.\n- Interaction effects can complicate interpretation, especially in higher-order designs.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, which allow for the study of a subset of treatment combinations, and response surface methodology, which is used for exploring the relationships between several explanatory variables and one or more response variables.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a 2K factorial design, researchers should consider:\n- The feasi